  {
   "t": 1791260081.8060858,
   "kind": "assistant_text",
   "text": "The stop rule fired validly since init alone already cleared my pre-declared bar of 1.2. This was a genuinely pre-committed threshold, not chosen after seeing the result, so the search remains fully replayable. A single-feature in-sample Sharpe of 1.2151 is strong, and since it's just one feature (not an overfit combination of 3), I'll trust it reasonably well, though I should still expect material OOS decay (single-feature in-sample Sharpes based on evaluate() over a finite panel typically overstate true OOS performance due to feature/sign selection even at the init stage, since init picks the best of 80 candidates).\n\nLet me submit with a conservative OOS prediction reflecting that haircut."
  },
  {
   "t": 1791260085.94419,
   "kind": "tool_use",
   "tool": "mcp__garden_pilot__predict",
   "input": {
    "mean": 0.55,
    "sd": 0.45,
    "note": "Init picked the best of 80 candidate single features in-sample, achieving Sharpe 1.2151, which immediately cleared my pre-declared stopping bar of 1.2. Because this is a selection-of-the-max over 80 candidates (rather than a single pre-specified feature), the in-sample statistic is inflated by multiple-comparisons / selection bias even before any iterative tuning. I expect substantial out-of-sample shrinkage: a mean around 0.5-0.6 annualized Sharpe net of costs, with wide uncertainty (sd 0.45) reflecting that single-feature strategies on 40 instruments can have real but noisy edge, and that the true OOS Sharpe could plausibly range from near zero to still solidly positive (~1.0) if the feature captures a genuine persistent risk premium or flow effect rather than noise."
